/-!
## The restricted Burnside problem

The restricted Burnside problem asks: for fixed `m` and `n`, is there a common bound on the
order of every finite group that is generated by `m` elements and has exponent dividing `n`?
Equivalently, are there only finitely many isomorphism classes of such groups?  Zelmanov's
theorem (Fields Medal, 1994), building on the Hall–Higman reduction to prime powers and the
classification of finite simple groups, answers this affirmatively.

Below, `RestrictedBurnsideBounded m n` is the order-bound formulation for the pair `(m, n)`,
`RestrictedBurnsideBoundedComm m n` is its restriction to abelian groups, and
`RestrictedBurnsideProblem` is the full statement.  Mathlib contains no form of the restricted
Burnside problem (a search for `Burnside` turns up only unrelated material, such as the
orbit-counting lemma), so the notions are set up here from scratch.

The target theorem `Frontier.zelmanov_restricted_burnside` collects what is proved here in
full, without any appeal to Zelmanov's theorem:

* the base case `n = 1` (trivial groups),
* the base case `m = 1` (cyclic groups: the bound is `n` itself),
* the **abelian case in full generality**: an abelian group generated by `m` elements and of
  exponent dividing `n > 0` has order at most `n ^ m`,
* the base case `n = 2` (elementary abelian `2`-groups: the bound is `2 ^ m`, Burnside's
  original observation),
* the reduction in the number of generators (`m' ≤ m`),
* the reduction along divisors of the exponent (`d ∣ n`).

Proving `RestrictedBurnsideProblem` in general would require formalizing Zelmanov's proof; it
is stated here as a definition, and deliberately not asserted.
-/

/-- `RestrictedBurnsideBounded m n` : there is a bound `B` such that every finite group
generated by at most `m` elements, all of whose elements satisfy `g ^ n = 1`, has order at
most `B`.  (Quantifying over groups in `Type` is no loss of generality: every finite group is
isomorphic to one carried by a type in `Type 0`.) -/

theorem restrictedBurnsideBounded_one_generator (n : ℕ) (hn : 0 < n) :
    RestrictedBurnsideBounded 1 n := by
  refine ⟨n, ?_⟩
  intro G _ _ S hS hgen hexp
  rcases S.eq_empty_or_nonempty with rfl | ⟨a, ha⟩
  · have htop : (⊤ : Subgroup G) = ⊥ := by simpa using hgen.symm
    have h1 : ∀ x : G, x = 1 := by
      intro x
      simpa using (Subgroup.eq_bot_iff_forall ⊤).1 htop x (by trivial)
    have : Subsingleton G := ⟨fun a b => by rw [h1 a, h1 b]⟩
    exact le_trans (Finite.card_le_one_iff_subsingleton.2 this) hn
  · have hSa : S = {a} := Finset.eq_singleton_iff_unique_mem.2
      ⟨ha, fun b hb => Finset.card_le_one.1 hS b hb a ha⟩
    subst hSa
    have hzp : Subgroup.zpowers a = ⊤ := by
      simpa [Subgroup.zpowers_eq_closure] using hgen
    have hcard : Nat.card G = orderOf a := by
      rw [← Nat.card_zpowers a, hzp]
      simp
    have hdvd : orderOf a ∣ n := orderOf_dvd_of_pow_eq_one (hexp a)
    rw [hcard]
    exact Nat.le_of_dvd hn hdvd

/-! ### The abelian case in full generality -/

/-- An abelian group of exponent dividing `n > 0`, generated by a finite set `S`, has order at
most `n ^ S.card`.

Written additively, such a group is a module over `ZMod n` spanned by `S`, so it is a quotient
of the free `ZMod n`-module on `S`, which has exactly `n ^ S.card` elements. -/
